, task ordering, split, extraction code \u2014 is bit-identical across the two conditions. Any accuracy difference is therefore attributable to the alignment criterion alone. This is strictly stronger than running two separate end-to-end evaluations that differ only in judge, because those could differ spuriously via generation nondeterminism or pipeline drift; here there is a single artifact set.\n\n**What the design can support:** a precisely scoped claim \u2014 \"under the specified \u03c4, tie-break, and normalization choices, MinED reports a higher alignment accuracy than EM on these 27 BBH tasks (aggregate / per-task breakdown), and the effect is/is not robust across \u03c4 and consistent across tasks.\"\n**What it cannot support:** any claim about the generative quality of the backbone; any claim generalizable to other tasks/models/outputs beyond the stored artifact set; claims that a criterion makes a model \"better\" at solving BBH in some intrinsic sense. The verdict is conditional on the specific outputs on which the criteria are applied.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Setup (pseudocode-level detail)\n\n### 2.1 Stage 1 \u2014 Generation (held FIXED, executed once, outputs stored)\n```\nFIXED = { model=MODEL            # ASSUMPTION: unspecified backbone (see \u00a76)\n          temperature=0.0, seed=SEED, max_tokens=MAX_TOKENS,\n          prompt_template=TPL, few_shot_exemplars=EX, split='test' }\n\nraw = {}                                 # single source of truth for scoring\nfor task in BBH_TASKS:                   # 27 tasks = 23 MC + 4 free-form (ASSUMPTION on identity)\n    prompts = build_prompts(task, EX, TPL)\n    for ex in load(task, 'test'):\n        raw[task][ex.id] = model.generate(prompts[ex.id], **FIXED)\n\nsave(raw)                                # BOTH criteria later read exactly this store\n```\nNo sampling; temperature 0 \u2192 deterministic generation (ASSUMPTION; if sampling is allowed, replicate over K seeds and pool stored outputs \u2014 flagged in \u00a76).\n\n### 2.2 Shared extraction + normalization (FIXED, applied identically BEFORE both criteria)\n```\ndef normalize_freeform(t):             # free-form tasks\n    t = t.strip()\n    t = re.sub(r'\\s+', ' ', t)\n    if NORM_LOWER: t = t.lower()       # NORM_LOWER=True for primary run (sensitivity on/off)\n    if NORM_PUNCT: t = t.translate(remove_punct)   # sensitivity on/off\n    return t\n\ndef preprocess(raw_out, task_type):\n    if task_type == 'MC':\n        letter = extract_answer_letter(raw_out)   # regexes for \"Answer: C\", \"(C)\", \"C\" at end\n        return (letter if letter else normalize_freeform(raw_out)), 'letter'\n    else:\n        return normalize_freeform(raw_out), 'text'\n```\n**Key control:** `preprocess` is a single shared function executed identically for the EM and MinED conditions; it cannot favor one criterion.\n\n### 2.3 EM \u2014 Exact Matching (reference criterion)\n```\ndef EM(pred, gold):\n    if pred is None or pred == '':      # failed/empty extraction -> mismatch\n        return 0.0\n    return 1.0 if pred == gold else 0.0\n```\n- MC: `pred` and `gold` are option letters (letter equality \u2014 ASSUMPTION, \u00a76); EM here is the standard BBH letter-match convention.\n- Free-form: exact string equality between normalized predicted text and the gold target string.\n\n### 2.4 MinED \u2014 Minimum Edit Distance (proposed criterion)\n```\ndef levenshtein(a, b):                  # pure-Python DP; primary = char-level\n    # standard O(|a|\u00b7|b|) dynamic program over characters\n    # (stdlib-only; difflib.SequenceMatcher allowed as an alternative distance source)\n\ndef min_edit_distance(pred, candidates):\n    ds = [levenshtein(pred, c) for c in candidates]\n    dn = [d / max(len(pred), len(c), 1) for d, c in zip(ds, candidates)]\n    i_min = min(range(len(dn)), key=lambda i: (dn[i], i))  # first-lowest canonical order\n    unique = (sorted(dn)[0] < sorted(dn)[1]) if len(dn) > 1 else True   # tie detection\n    return i_min, dn[i_min], unique\n\ndef MinED(pred, candidates, \u03c4):\n    if pred is None or pred == '': return 0.0                    # same failed-extraction rule as EM\n    i, d_norm, unique = min_edit_distance(pred, candidates)\n    if TIE_RULE == 'conservative' and not unique:\n        return 0.0                                               # ambiguous nearest -> reject\n    if TIE_RULE == 'first':\n        pass                                                     # keep i_min\n    return 1.0 if d_norm <= \u03c4 else 0.0\n```\n- **Candidate enumeration**\n  - *MC (23 tasks):* candidates = the 5 option strings `{A: opt_A, \u2026, E: opt_E}`; the predicted letter is the option minimizing distance; correctness = `d_norm \u2264 \u03c4` (letter label matches option text internally, so on near-identical option text MinED converges to letter selection \u2014 the placebo property, \u00a73).\n  - *Free-form (4 tasks):* candidates = the gold target set(s) for that example. If an example has **multiple valid gold targets** (e.g., any valid Dyck string of length n; a correctly sorted word list), correctness = `min over all gold targets of d_norm \u2264 \u03c4`. Candidate sets are built from gold labels only (\u00a73 leakage guard).\n- **\u03c4 sweep grid (sensitivity):** `\u03c4 \u2208 {0.0, 0.05, 0.1, 0.15 (primary), 0.2, 0.3}`. \u03c4 = 0 with exact equality \u2261 EM, giving a continuity/self-consistency check that the two implementations agree when they should.\n- **Distance-variant sweep (sensitivity):** char-level Levenshtein (primary) vs word-token-level Levenshtein (split on whitespace) vs a `difflib.SequenceMatcher` ratio-based closeness \u2265 \u03c1 (mapped to a distance).\n\n### 2.5 Hyperparameters \u2014 fixed vs swept (explicit)\n**Fixed:** model, prompt template + exemplars, decoding (temp 0, seed, max tokens), task ordering, split, shared extraction/normalization, primary \u03c4=0.15, char-level Levenshtein, conservative tie-break, NORM_LOWER=True/NORM_PUNCT=False.\n**Swept (sensitivity only, never alters the shared generation):** \u03c4 \u2208 grid; char-vs-word-vs-difflib distance; tie-break (conservative vs first); NORM_LOWER / NORM_PUNCT on/off.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Controls and safeguards\n\n1. **Held identical across conditions (bit-for-bit):** the stored raw outputs (one artifact set), the shared `preprocess`/normalization function, gold answer sets, task ordering, dataset split. Only `EM()` vs `MinED()` differ. Any implementation bug is thereby symmetric unless it is inside the matching function itself.\n2. **Leakage guards:** MinED candidate sets are constructed **from gold labels only** (option text for MC; gold targets for free-form) \u2014 never from any model output or from other examples; few-shot exemplars disjoint from test examples; scorers see one example at a time (no cross-example information).\n3. **MC placebo check (sanity of the pipeline):** on the 23 MC tasks MinED and EM should produce near-identical aggregate accuracy (both collapse to letter selection). **Pre-registered rule:** if |macro(MC, MinED) \u2212 macro(MC, EM)| > \u03b5 = 1 accuracy point, flag a pipeline/extraction bug and do not issue a clean verdict until resolved.\n4. **Free-form false-accept guard:** enumerate examples MinED accepts but EM rejects; manually spot-check the first ~50 (sorted by distance) and classify each as (a) genuine near-miss that an expert would credit vs (b) genuinely wrong answer let in by a lenient \u03c4. Report the **false-accept rate** = fraction of (b). If the false-accept rate is high (e.g., >20% of MinED-only accepts), the free-form \"gain\" is discounted in the verdict.\n5. **\u03c4-inflation guard:** the verdict must hold at the primary \u03c4=0.15 and remain directionally stable across the plausible \u03c4 range; a win that exists only at \u03c4 \u2265 0.2 is reported as non-robust (\u00a74 partial-branch b).\n6. **Prompt-order / carry-over:** single-pass generation with no sequential dependency between conditions; scoring is deterministic and order-independent.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Analysis plan\n\n### 4.1 Outputs\n- **Per-task table (primary result artifact):** rows = 27 tasks grouped MC (23) then free-form (4); columns = `EM_acc`, `MinED_acc`, `\u0394`, direction (MinED wins / loses / ties). Plus the manual spot-check classification for the free-form false-accept guard.\n- **Aggregates:** **macro-average** accuracy (mean of 27 per-task accuracies) = primary aggregate; micro-average (all examples pooled) reported secondarily; both for EM and MinED.\n- **Paired inference (all on the identical artifact set, so paired by construction):**\n  - *Paired bootstrap CI:* resample the 27 tasks with replacement (10,000 resamples, pure-stdlib `random`), CI on macro(MinED)\u2212macro(EM); report that CI excludes 0 or not.\n  - *Sign test* across the 27 tasks (MinED>EM / MinED<EM / ties), both-sided p-value.\n  - *McNemar-style per-example test:* per task, 2\u00d72 contingency (correct under EM-only vs MinED-only vs both vs neither); aggregate McNemar over pooled examples as a secondary signal.\n- **Sensitivity:** repeat aggregate + per-task under (i) \u03c4 grid, (ii) char-vs-word-vs-difflib distance, (iii) tie-break variants, (iv) normalization on/off. Report whether the primary verdict survives the plausible hyperparameter range.\n\n### 4.2 Pre-registered verdict rules (incl. partial conditions)\nDefine `G = macro(MinED) \u2212 macro(EM)` at primary \u03c4=0.15.\n\n- **Full \"MinED outperforms EM\":** all of \u2014 (a) G > 0; (b) bootstrap 95% CI on G excludes 0; (c) MinED \u2265 EM on \u2265 24/27 tasks (sign-test consistency); (d) MC placebo holds (|\u0394MC| \u2264 \u03b5); (e) free-form gain survives the false-accept spot-check.\n- **Partial branches (how the verdict is worded when only some conditions hold):**\n  - *a) Aggregate gain, weaker consistency:* (a)(b) hold but task majority < 24/27 \u2192 \"MinED outperforms EM **on aggregate**, but the advantage is **not consistent across tasks**; it is concentrated in {list of tasks with \u0394>0}.\"\n  - *b) Threshold-dependence:* gain exists but only at \u03c4 \u2265 0.2 \u2192 \"MinED outperforms EM **only under a lenient threshold**; not robust at primary \u03c4.\"\n  - *c) Free-form-only:* gain located entirely in the 4 free-form tasks with \u0394MC \u2248 0 \u2192 \"MinED outperforms EM **on the free-form tasks** and is **neutral** on MC tasks.\"\n  - *d) False-accept contamination:* free-form gain coincides with high false-accept rate \u2192 the gain is **not credited**; verdict downgraded to \"no evidence MinED improves **measured correctness** (it improves lenient acceptance).\"\n  - *e) No gain or CI includes 0* \u2192 \"MinED **does not outperform** EM under this design.\"\nThese branches make the verdict falsifiable and force an explicit statement of *where* any advantage comes from (MC vs free-form) rather than a single opaque number.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Feasibility\n\n- **Fully feasible as a design in this environment.** The deliverable requires no execution, and the study context explicitly waives repositories and compute. Runtimes/compute are not required and none are claimed.\n- **Implementation feasibility of the spec is verified conceptually against the environment:** the environment is stdlib-only (confirmed: no `numpy`/`scipy`/`Levenshtein`/`editdistance`; `difflib` available; Python 3.14.4). The design therefore specifies a pure-Python DP Levenshtein and `difflib.SequenceMatcher` alternatives, and bootstrap/sign-test/McNemar computations are expressible with `random`/`math`/`statistics` alone. A smoke check that the *scoring functions* compile and yield sane distances on toy strings could be run in an optional scaffolding step but would be a feasibility sanity check only \u2014 **not** an experiment and **not** evidence of any BBH result.\n- **Remaining external dependency:** generating the 27-task BBH outputs requires model access and the BBH dataset, which are out of scope for this design-only task and explicitly not required; the design assumes their availability at execution time (flagged as an assumption).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Assumptions and uncertainties (explicitly labeled, not facts)\n\n**Assumptions (may materially affect the verdict):**\n1. **Backbone model unspecified** (e.g., PaLM/code-davinci-002 as in the original BBH paper vs a current open-weights model). Affects absolute accuracy and generalizability; does **not** compromise the relative criterion comparison because scoring is post-hoc on identical outputs.\n2. **Identity of the 4 free-form tasks** not visible in this workspace (e.g., candidates like dyck_languages, word_sorting, multistep_arithmetic). Affects candidate enumeration and, potentially, the size of the free-form effect.\n3. **EM-for-MC = option-letter equality** after extraction (standard BBH convention), not full-string equality. Affects the MC placebo baseline.\n4. **\"Outperforms\" = higher alignment accuracy** (not efficiency/robustness-only).\n5. **\u03c4=0.15 and the conservative tie-break as primary choices** (not source-specified); their effect is bounded by the sensitivity sweep.\n6. **Single-pass, temperature-0 generation**; if sampling at execution time, this becomes K seeded passes and an extra seed-sensitivity analysis.\n\n**Uncertainties (design handles but cannot resolve from available sources):**\n- Whether the source defines MinED as *thresholded acceptance* (this design) vs *argmin-only without threshold* \u2014 changes the false-accept profile on free-form tasks.\n- Whether free-form gold answers are single or multiple valid targets per example \u2014 handled by \"min over gold set\" candidate enumeration, but the multiplicity itself is unverified.\n- Exact normalization convention (lowercase/punctuation) not pinned by sources \u2014 covered by the on/off sensitivity.\n- Power of a 27-task sign test with ties \u2014 why the design adds per-example McNemar and the bootstrap CI as co-signals.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of the experimental protocol (one-paragraph executable spec)\nGenerate all 27 BBH test outputs once with a fixed, deterministic pipeline and store them; apply a single shared extraction/normalization; score every stored example twice \u2014 EM (exact equality; letter equality on the 23 MC tasks) and MinED (normalized Levenshtein to the gold/option candidate set, nearest-candidate acceptance at \u03c4=0.15, conservative tie-break) \u2014 while holding everything else bit-identical; report per-task accuracy, macro/micro aggregates, paired-bootstrap CI, sign test, and per-example McNemar; sweep \u03c4 \u2208 {0,\u2026,0.3}, char-vs-word distance, tie-break, and normalization to establish robustness; enforce the MC placebo check and free-form false-accept spot-check; and emit the verdict via the pre-registered full/partial-branch rules. Execution is out of scope for this deliverable.", "evidence": "Workspace inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (documents: track.md = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"); no BBH dataset, model checkpoints, or source code exist to inspect, so the design rests on the researcher-anchored scope (27 BBH tasks = 23 MC + 4 free-form; MinED vs EM alignment criteria) plus explicitly flagged assumptions. Environment check: Python 3.14.4 available; imports of Levenshtein, editdistance, numpy, and scipy all failed (stdlib-only), while difflib is available \u2014 motivating the pure-Python DP Levenshtein / difflib.SequenceMatcher implementation spec in \u00a72.4 and the stdlib-only statistical procedures in \u00a74 (random/math/statistics). No experiment was run and no results are claimed; the scaffold-only feasibility note in \u00a75 is labeled as such.", "assumptions": ["Backbone model and its weights are unspecified by the sources; affects absolute accuracy and generalizability but not the relative criterion comparison (post-hoc scoring on identical outputs).", "The 4 free-form BBH tasks' identity is not verifiable in this workspace (e.g., dyck_languages/word_sorting etc.); affects candidate enumeration for MinED.", "EM on the 23 MC tasks is defined as option-letter equality after extraction (standard BBH convention), not full-string equality.", "'MinED outperforms EM' is interpreted as higher alignment accuracy, not efficiency or robustness alone.", "Primary MinED hyperparameters (\u03c4=0.15, conservative tie-break, char-level Levenshtein, NORM_LOWER=True) are design choices not pinned by sources, bounded by the sensitivity sweep.", "Single-pass, temperature-0, fixed-seed generation is assumed unless execution-time sampling is allowed (then K seeds + seed-sensitivity analysis).", "At execution time, model access and the BBH dataset are assumed available though explicitly not required for this design-only deliverable."], "uncertainties": ["Whether the source defines MinED as thresholded acceptance (as designed) vs argmin-only without a threshold, which changes the free-form false-accept profile.", "Whether free-form gold answers are single or multiple valid targets per example (design covers the multiple case via 'min over gold set' enumeration, but multiplicity is unverified).", "Exact normalization convention (lowercasing/punctuation) is not pinned; covered by the on/off sensitivity runs.", "Statistical power of a 27-task sign test with ties \u2014 mitigated by adding per-example McNemar and the paired bootstrap CI as co-signals in the pre-registered rule."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-e6f62ae2576f48fcb675de08ce3fc6cd", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver final experiment design MinED vs EM on BBH", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
